  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1200 PM CDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\nA frontal boundary that extends from near IA/IL southeastward\nacross the southern Great Lakes into the Mid-Upper Ohio Valley\nwill be the focusing mechanism for daily storm chances thru the\nend of the week. For today, an up to 12C H7 cap keeps storm \nchances isolated here, at best, as a first wave of energy blows \nup an MCS-like complex of convection on its track that is displaced\nwell off to our north and east. The effect this has on the \nboundary placement is to shift it a little further southward \nover time, so that tmrw's similar wave of energy that tracks \nsimilarly then shoots better pops/svr risk into and across our \nFA. It will be subject to the boundary's ultimate placement, and\nwar against the still capped mid levels, but climbing bulk \nshear and steepening lapse rates in the vicinity of the wave's \npunch this direction will have the super-rich/steep gradient of \nMUCAPE to tap, with damaging wind potential the main anticipated\nsevere hazard late Wednesday PM/evening and into the overnight;\nan isolated tor (2% risk) tapping the 200-400 m2/s2 srh modeled\nnear the boundary cannot be ruled out. High PW's will of course\nprovide a locally heavy rain potential hazard with any stronger\nstorms as well.\n\nConfidence decreases after Wednesday, given 2 days worth of\nboundary adjustment, but generally, we warm sector again and\nchances shift northward and eastward before essentially capping\noff by the weekend as the broad, upper high domes us again. We\nare talking additional or extended heat headlines that may run\nthru the weekend eventually, but thus far this early,\ncollaborative efforts appear centered on a steady-as-she goes\npersistence with the headline running thru Thursday and\nrevisited later for headline timing and spacial adjustment \nconsiderations.",
